    "kicker": "ARABIC101 / CHAPTER 03",
    "title": "Long vowels, made simple.",
    "subtitle": "A visual guide to recognizing, reading and writing Arabic long vowels · Book pp. 87-92",
    "coreIdeaLabel": "THE ONE RULE TO REMEMBER",
    "coreIdea": "Match the **short vowel** to the next **long-vowel letter**. Extend the sound to about **two beats**.",
    "coreIdeaNote": "The long-vowel letter is not pronounced as an additional consonant or syllable.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Three vowel pairs",
        "bookPages": "87",
        "note": "Look at the sign on the previous letter",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "cards",
            "columns": 3,
            "items": [
              {
                "title": "FATḤAH + ALIF",
                "kicker": "a -> aa",
                "arFrom": "بَ",
                "ar": "بَا",
                "caption": "ba · 1 beat  ->  **baa · 2 beats**",
                "note": "The alif ا has no vowel mark of its own."
              },
              {
                "title": "ḌAMMAH + WAAW",
                "kicker": "u -> uu",
                "arFrom": "بُ",
                "ar": "بُو",
                "caption": "bu · 1 beat  ->  **buu · 2 beats**",
                "note": "The waaw و has no vowel mark of its own."
              },
              {
                "title": "KASRAH + YAAʔ",
                "kicker": "i -> ii",
                "arFrom": "بِ",
                "ar": "بِي",
                "caption": "bi · 1 beat  ->  **bii · 2 beats**",
                "note": "The yaaʔ ي has no vowel mark of its own."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Is it really a long vowel?",
        "bookPages": "87",
        "note": "Use this quick three-step test",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "steps",
            "items": [
              {
                "title": "Find",
                "text": "ا / و / ي in the word (never as its first letter)."
              },
              {
                "title": "Look right in Arabic",
                "text": "Does the letter before it carry the matching mark: fatḥah before ا, ḍammah before و, kasrah before ي?"
              },
              {
                "title": "Check the letter",
                "text": "It has no mark of its own -> extend the earlier vowel."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Waaw and yaaʔ can also be consonants",
        "bookPages": "91-92",
        "note": "They do NOT always mean uu / ii",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "cards",
            "columns": 2,
            "items": [
              {
                "title": "Waaw و · /uu/ or /w/ ?",
                "rows": [
                  {
                    "label": "Long vowel -- preceding ḍammah",
                    "ar": "رُوحُ",
                    "translit": "ruu ḥu"
                  },
                  {
                    "label": "Consonant -- its own fatḥah",
                    "ar": "وَلَدْ",
                    "translit": "wa lad"
                  }
                ],
                "note": "A fatḥah + وْ makes **aw** (as in مَوْلِدَ /maw li da/) -- not long uu."
              },
              {
                "title": "Yaaʔ ي · /ii/ or /y/ ?",
                "rows": [
                  {
                    "label": "Long vowel -- preceding kasrah",
                    "ar": "فِيلُ",
                    "translit": "fii lu"
                  },
                  {
                    "label": "Consonant -- its own fatḥah",
                    "ar": "يَأْكُلُ",
                    "translit": "yaʔ ku lu"
                  }
                ],
                "note": "A fatḥah + يْ makes **ay** (as in بَيْعُ /bay ʿu/) -- not long ii."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Two easy-to-miss distinctions",
        "bookPages": "89-90",
        "note": "From book pp. 89-90",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "cards",
            "columns": 2,
            "items": [
              {
                "title": "Alif ا versus alif with hamzah أ",
                "caption": "قَالَ = **qaa la** (long vowel); ثَأْرَ = **thaʔ ra** (hamzah sound ʔ).",
                "note": "Don't treat أ as an ordinary long alif: ثَارَ /thaa ra/ (to rebel) is a different word from ثَأْرَ (revenge)."
              },
              {
                "title": "End-of-word alif maqsuurah ى",
                "caption": "هُدَى is **hu daa**, not \"hu day\". The undotted ى is a long aa here; it is not the same as dotted ي.",
                "note": "Same sound as ا, but these words are always spelled with ى: عَلَى, أَذَى, هُدَى."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Hear the length, not an extra syllable",
        "bookPages": "88",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "cards",
            "columns": 2,
            "items": [
              {
                "title": "SHORT",
                "ar": "بَ",
                "translit": "ba",
                "caption": "**1 beat** · short a"
              },
              {
                "title": "LONG",
                "ar": "بَا",
                "translit": "baa",
                "caption": "**2 beats** · one syllable · long aa"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "warn",
            "title": "Avoid this mistake:",
            "text": "بَا is **baa** -- not **ba + ʔa**. The long vowel extends the existing sound; it is not a second spoken letter."
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "exercisePages": "93-97",
